23:45:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: That’s a great question—it seems odd because you don’t swallow the gum itself! The key is that while the rubbery gum base is indigestible and passes through your system, gum contains other ingredients that *do* get swallowed and digested. When you chew gum, the sweeteners (sugar, glucose syrup, or sugar alcohols like sorbitol/xylitol), flavorings, and any other soluble components dissolve in your saliva. You inevitably swallow that saliva, taking those dissolved ingredients along with it. Those soluble parts contain the calories. Think of it like sucking on a hard candy—even though you don’t eat the wrapper, you’re still consuming the sugar that dissolves off. With gum, you’re just extracting the caloric ingredients by chewing instead of sucking, then swallowing them. Sugar-free gums use calorie-free or low-calorie sweeteners, so their calorie count is often negligible, but any gum with real sugar will give you a small dose of calories from the sugar you swallow. So in a way, you *do* eat part of the gum—just not the rubbery base!
